      "completion_tokens": 612,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through chemical, physical, and ecological lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Definition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is famous for being uniquely weird compared to other liquids. Its physical properties make it the foundation of life:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the way its atoms bond (giving it a slight electrical charge, making it a \"polar\" molecule), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries nutrients through our bloodstreams and minerals through plant roots.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet, as well as the internal temperature of living bodies.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n* **Global Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh water. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Earth's water is constantly recycled through evaporation, condensation, precipitation (rain and snow), and collection. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n* It acts as a cushion for organs and joints, regulates body temperature (through sweating), and flushes out waste.\n* Plants use water, sunlight, and carbon dioxide to make their own food through **photosynthesis**, which also produces the oxygen we breathe.\n\nIn short, water is a simple chemical combination that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
